## The Problem: Object Reference Equality

React uses **shallow comparison** (`===`) to determine if props changed. When you pass an object as a prop, React compares **references**, not values.

```javascript
// This creates a NEW object on every render, even if values are identical
<MyComponent config={{ theme: 'dark', size: 'large' }} />
```

Every parent render creates a new object literal → new reference → React thinks props changed → child re-renders.

---

## Why This Happens

```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ❌ New object reference created on every render
  const config = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <Child config={config} /> {/* Re-renders every time count changes */}
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Solutions

### 1. `useMemo` — Memoize the object
```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ✅ Same reference unless dependencies change
  const config = useMemo(() => ({ 
    theme: 'dark', 
    size: 'large' 
  }), []); // Add actual dependencies here

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

### 2. Move the object outside the component
```javascript
// ✅ Defined once, never recreated (best for static data)
const CONFIG = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

function Parent() {
  return <Child config={CONFIG} />;
}
```

### 3. `React.memo` on the child — Skip re-renders
```javascript
// ✅ Child only re-renders if props actually changed
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});
```

> **Note:** `React.memo` still uses shallow comparison, so it won't help if you're passing new object literals — combine it with `useMemo`.

### 4. Pass primitive values instead of objects
```javascript
// ✅ Primitives compare by value, not reference
<Child theme="dark" size="large" />
```

---

## Choosing the Right Solution

| Situation | Solution |
|---|---|
| Static config that never changes | Move outside component |
| Object depends on props/state | `useMemo` with correct dependencies |
| Want to protect a child component broadly | `React.memo` + `useMemo` |
| Object is small and simple | Pass as separate primitive props |

---

## Verifying the Fix

Use the **React DevTools Profiler** to confirm — it shows exactly why a component re-rendered (prop change, state change, parent render, etc.).

```javascript
// Quick debug: log when component renders and why
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  console.log('Child rendered');
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});
```

The most common mistake is reaching for `React.memo` alone without addressing the root cause — the new object reference being created on every render.
